WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:12.075
According to The Register in July 2025, a founder was building a project with an AI coding agent.

2
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:20.219
By his own account, he had explicitly said not to change code without permission, yet the agent deleted the production database.

3
00:00:20.569 --> 00:00:27.175
The agent had also faked data, and said the database couldn't be restored when in fact it could.

4
00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:33.906
He said he had told the agent eleven times, in all caps: don't do this.

5
00:00:34.506 --> 00:00:42.321
That's his account. But it's a reminder: telling an agent not to do something doesn't mean it can't.

6
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.684
This is C7: the age of agents, delegation and supervision.

7
00:00:49.034 --> 00:00:57.420
A chat AI gives you answers; an agent acts for you. Actions have consequences. How do you hand work over, and how do you keep watch?

8
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:07.216
What is an agent? You give it a job; it breaks it into steps, uses tools, looks at the results, and decides what to do next.

9
00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:15.935
Anthropic's engineering article says it's often just a large model using tools in a loop, based on feedback from its environment.

10
00:01:16.285 --> 00:01:24.269
Good designs make it pause for a human at checkpoints, and set stopping conditions, such as a maximum number of steps.

11
00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:37.225
But autonomy also means one wrong step can lead to more. The article recommends thorough testing in a sandbox before real use, plus guardrails.

12
00:01:37.825 --> 00:01:48.825
Another risk comes from outside: instructions hidden in a web page or email that trick the AI into doing something you didn't ask. That's prompt injection.

13
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.582
So before handing anything over, ask four questions.

14
00:01:54.132 --> 00:02:07.313
Is it small? Start with a small job. Can it be undone? Drafting and sorting can be redone; payments, sending and deleting can't.

15
00:02:07.663 --> 00:02:15.629
Can you say what 'done' looks like? Are the permissions just enough? Connect only the tools and accounts this job needs.

16
00:02:15.979 --> 00:02:23.567
OpenAI's own usage tips say the same: avoid vague instructions like 'check my email and handle everything'.

17
00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:31.144
Next, set human checkpoints. For these five kinds of action, it must stop and ask you first.

18
00:02:31.494 --> 00:02:42.508
Spending money; sending anything out in your name; deleting or overwriting; changing accounts or permissions; and anything else that can't be undone.

19
00:02:43.108 --> 00:02:52.685
Add a stop rule: if it does something you didn't ask for, asks for your password or code, or you can't tell what it's doing, stop it at once.

20
00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:59.658
Supervising isn't staring at the screen; it's keeping a record. That's the supervision log.

21
00:03:00.008 --> 00:03:09.583
A demo: ask an agent to turn 12 book-club sign-up forms into one table, then draft a group notice, and show me before sending.

22
00:03:09.933 --> 00:03:19.517
Step 2: the table has only 11 rows, so I have it add the missing one. Step 4: the notice says Saturday instead of Sunday, so I have it fixed.

23
00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:31.028
Step 5 is posting to the residents' group: a checkpoint, so I post it myself. Step 6: it offers to share the table with everyone; it has phone numbers, so I stop it.

24
00:03:31.378 --> 00:03:36.945
What goes in the log is the evidence you saw yourself, not a copy of its report.

25
00:03:37.734 --> 00:03:46.328
Remember: small jobs, small permissions; five kinds of action ask a human first; keep a supervision log as you go.

26
00:03:46.678 --> 00:03:53.286
Quick exercise: give AI a small job and write a supervision log of at least five steps.

27
00:03:53.636 --> 00:03:59.836
Next time, the final episode: C8, when not to use AI.
